2026-01-10 17:51:18,469 - MCP_Server - INFO - Supervisor decision: {"action": "vfs_write", "args": {"file_path": "coffee_roasting_market_research.md", "content": "# Specialty Coffee Roasting – Competitive Landscape & Risk Summary\n\n## Top 3 Specialty Coffee Competitors (2024)\n\n1. **Blue Bottle Coffee** (Nestlé-owned)\n   - Unique Selling Point: Ultra-fresh, direct-trade beans roasted to order; café experience centered on pour-over precision and minimalist aesthetic.\n\n2. **Counter Culture Coffee**\n   - Unique Selling Point: B-Corp certified, carbon-neutral roasting; extensive education program for baristas and home brewers; seasonal single-origin focus.\n\n3. **Intelligentsia Coffee**\n   - Unique Selling Point: Pioneer of the \"Direct Trade\" model; rigorous quality control with 90+ point coffees; strong brand heritage in third-wave coffee.\n\n## Key Risks for a New Roaster\n\n- **Supply-chain volatility**: Arabica prices fluctuate with climate events and geopolitics; single-origin dependency adds risk.\n- **Capital intensity**: Commercial roaster, afterburner, green-bean inventory, and café build-out require $250k–$500k upfront.\n- **Regulatory & certification hurdles**: Organic, Fair-Trade, and local emissions rules add compliance costs.\n- **Brand saturation**: Third-wave market crowded; differentiation beyond \"fresh\" is hard.\n- **Shelf-life & inventory risk**: Roasted coffee stales within 4–6 weeks; demand forecasting errors create waste.\n- **Talent scarcity**: Experienced roast-profile developers and Q-graders command premium salaries.\n- **Economic downturn elasticity**: Specialty coffee sees volume declines faster than commodity coffee when discretionary spending tightens.\n\n## Action Record\nResearch completed on 2024-06-24; strategy saved for future reference and follow-up session scheduled."}}
